  {
    "episode_id": 77650,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77650.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Part of me said yes, yes, yes, let's do whatever it takes. Let's go to war. The other part of me said, you were elected to rise above this and especially in moments of national security. You know, the public, quite frankly, are really very emotional about, but they elected us to try to sort all this out for them and to be a member of Congress who can lead. And in that moment, I said, no, this is so important, so serious. I cannot just go with the flow. I've got to clarify what I think is taking place as it relates to this resolution and why this could lead us to a war without end. There was a memorial service. I believe it was that, well, it was on the 14th, I believe. You know, members of Congress attended and I was not going to go because I wanted to stay on the Hill and talk it through with my staff what my vote should be on this resolution and talk to a few more people. But at the last minute I was in what we call the cloak room that's in back of the chamber and talking to one of my colleagues and I said, you know, I think I'm going to go to the memorial service. I probably was the last one on the bus. I raced down the steps.",
